# The default certificate: Caddy's own CA.
#
# A bureau's server has a name like `payroll.office.local` or no name at all,
# just an address on the LAN. Let's Encrypt cannot issue for either — it has to
# reach the host from the internet, which is the whole thing an on-prem install
# is avoiding. So Caddy runs a small certificate authority of its own and issues
# from it.
#
# That costs one step, once: each staff machine has to trust the CA, or the
# browser shows a warning. The certificate is written to the `caddy-data`
# volume; export it with
#
#     docker compose cp caddy:/data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt .
#
# and install it as a trusted root. The README has the per-platform steps.
#
# The alternative is plain HTTP, and plain HTTP does not work here: all three
# apps mark the session cookie `Secure` in production, and a browser silently
# discards a Secure cookie that arrived over http://. The login page would take
# the password, redirect, and come back to itself for ever.

{
	# Nothing is fetched from a public CA in this mode, so the ACME machinery is
	# left switched off rather than failing in the background every few minutes.
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

https://{$SITE_ADDRESS}:{$CONSOLE_PORT} {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy console:3201
}

https://{$SITE_ADDRESS}:{$ADMIN_PORT} {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy admin:3300
}

https://{$SITE_ADDRESS}:{$PORTAL_PORT} {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy portal:3400
}
